  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nSaturday...Large east coast cyclone and blocked pattern acrs the \neastern 2/3's of the CONUS to hold up the passing trof acrs the \nregion on Saturday, and thus clouds with sctrd showers. Still expect \nmore activity and shower maintenance acrs IA and MO with lingering \nprecip erosion processes as you get further to the east acrs IL. \nThus better chance for at least some light rain accums more west of \nthe MS RVR. With drying easterly fetch continuing acrs IL and \npotentially more cloud/precip breaks there, we may have a temp \ngradient with warmer highs in the east and cooler highs only in the \nlow 60s in the far western CWA. Showery, misty night looks in store \nfor Saturday night with the upper trof lingering overhead.\n\nSunday and beyond...The signal is for the precipitation to wane \nSunday morning as mid-level ridging reinvigorates and weakens the \nshortwave forcing. With the cloud cover and rain in place, \ntemperatures will feel cooler than average, with highs in the 60s.\n\nSunday night through Monday appears likely to be a dry period thanks \nto high pressure dominating over the region. Partly to mostly sunny \nskies and southeasterly flow Monday should result in the return of \nupper 60s to lower 70s for high temperatures.\n\nThis period of dryness will come to an end mid-week as a larger \nsystem builds in sometime around late Tuesday/Wednesday. A longer \nwave trough, with models showing a strong tropical connection to the \nsystems in the eastern Pacific, should translate through the area, \nalthough the exact timing of associated precipitation remains \nuncertain. However, there is a strong signal for abundant moisture \nwith this system, with Pwat values of around 1.5 to 2 inches. \nExceedance probabilities of 1.6 inch Pwats are around 50-70% per the \nlatest LREF ensemble, supporting a period of heavy rainfall \npotential. Now, it remains unclear how long this period of rainfall \nwill last into Wednesday, given differing end times of precip per \nthe GFS/ECMWF/CMC global model solutions as some guidance shows a \nlarge progressive longer wave trough sweeping across the mid-CONUS \nversus a slower cut-off low with multiple rounds of rains mid to \nlate week. The latter scenario would be more concerning for heavy \nrainfall/flash flooding, given wet antecedent conditions, so this \nmid-week system does bear watching.",
